    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for caramelized beef bowls stacked with fresh vegetables. A Vietnamese restaurant charges $15 for the equivalent, and it will not come with this much cucumber.",
    "shop_smart":  ["The 93/7 ground beef is the anchor at 3.75 pounds. Lean beef matters here, the glaze needs to stick to meat, not swim in grease.","Cucumbers are the sneaky volume play, 14 of them keeping every bowl fresh and crunchy. Buy them loose, not shrink-wrapped, the loose ones run cheaper per pound.","Lemongrass paste in the tube costs more per ounce than fresh stalks, but there is zero waste and it keeps in the fridge for months. For a few teaspoons per batch, the tube wins on real cost."],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cook the rice per the package and set it aside.",
                    "Quick-pickle the vegetables: shred the carrots and thinly slice the red onion, then toss them with the apple cider vinegar, the sugar, and a pinch of the salt. Let them sit while you cook, stirring once or twice.",
                    "Prep the rest of the toppings: slice the cucumbers, halve the cherry tomatoes, and thinly slice the green onions.",
                    "Heat the vegetable oil in your largest skillet over high heat and brown the ground beef hard, breaking it up and seasoning with the remaining salt and the black pepper, until cooked through with crispy edges.",
                    "Add the garlic, jalapeno, and lemongrass paste and stir fry 1 minute.",
                    "Add the brown sugar, fish sauce, and soy sauce. Cook, stirring, until the sugar melts into a dark, glossy caramel that coats the beef, 3 to 5 minutes.",
                    "Fold in most of the green onions and pull it off the heat.",
                    "Build bowls: rice, caramelized beef, then the pickled and fresh vegetables on top with a sprinkle of toasted sesame seeds."
                ],
